..
.\"	# UL - underline word
.de UL
\\$1\l'|0\(ul'\\$2
..
.\"	# QW - apply quotation marks to word
.de QW
.ie '\\*(lq'"' ``\\$1''\\$2
.\"" fix emacs highlighting
.el \\*(lq\\$1\\*(rq\\$2
..
.\"	# PQ - apply parens and quotation marks to word
.de PQ
.ie '\\*(lq'"' (``\\$1''\\$2)\\$3
.\"" fix emacs highlighting
.el (\\*(lq\\$1\\*(rq\\$2)\\$3
..
.\"	# QR - quoted range
.de QR
.ie '\\*(lq'"' ``\\$1''\\-``\\$2''\\$3
.\"" fix emacs highlighting
.el \\*(lq\\$1\\*(rq\\-\\*(lq\\$2\\*(rq\\$3
..
.\"	# MT - "empty" string
.de MT
.QW ""
..
.BS
.SH NAME
ns_register \- Register Tcl/ADP handlers
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBns_register_adp\fR ?\fB-constraints \fIconstraints\fR\fR? ?\fB-noinherit\fR? ?\fB-expires \fItime\fR\fR? ?\fB-options autoabort|detailerror|displayerror|expire|cache|safe|singlescript|stricterror|trace|trimspace|stream\fR? ?--? \fImethod\fR \fIurl\fR ?\fIfile\fR?
.sp
\fBns_register_auth\fR ?\fB-authority \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-first\fR? ?--? request|user \fIscript\fR ?\fIarg \&.\&.\&.\fR?
.sp
\fBns_register_fastpath\fR ?\fB-constraints \fIconstraints\fR\fR? ?\fB-noinherit\fR? ?--? \fImethod\fR \fIurl\fR
.sp
\fBns_register_filter\fR ?\fB-constraints \fIconstraints\fR\fR? ?\fB-first\fR? ?--? preauth|postauth|trace \fImethod\fR \fIurlpattern\fR \fIscript\fR ?\fIarg \&.\&.\&.\fR?
.sp
\fBns_register_proc\fR ?\fB-constraints \fIconstraints\fR\fR? ?\fB-noinherit\fR? ?--? \fImethod\fR \fIurl\fR \fIscript\fR ?\fIarg \&.\&.\&.\fR?
.sp
\fBns_register_proxy\fR \fImethod\fR \fIprotocol\fR \fIscript\fR ?\fIarg \&.\&.\&.\fR?
.sp
\fBns_register_tcl\fR ?\fB-constraints \fIconstraints\fR\fR? ?\fB-noinherit\fR? ?--? \fImethod\fR \fIurl\fR ?\fIfile\fR?
.sp
\fBns_register_trace\fR ?\fB-constraints \fIconstraints\fR\fR? \fImethod\fR \fIurlpattern\fR \fIscript\fR ?\fIarg \&.\&.\&.\fR?
.sp
\fBns_register_url2file\fR ?\fB-noinherit\fR? ?--? \fIurl\fR \fIscript\fR ?\fIarg \&.\&.\&.\fR?
.sp
\fBns_register_fasturl2file\fR ?\fB-noinherit\fR? ?--? \fIurl\fR ?\fIbasepath\fR?
.sp
\fBns_shortcut_filter\fR ?\fB-constraints \fIconstraints\fR\fR? preauth|postauth|trace \fImethod\fR \fIurlpattern\fR
.sp
\fBns_unregister_op\fR ?\fB-allconstraints\fR? ?\fB-noinherit\fR? ?\fB-recurse\fR? ?\fB-server \fIserver\fR\fR? ?--? \fImethod\fR \fIurl\fR
.sp
\fBns_unregister_url2file\fR ?\fB-noinherit\fR? ?\fB-recurse\fR? ?\fB-server \fIserver\fR\fR? ?--? \fIurl\fR
.sp
.BE
.SH DESCRIPTION
This document describes various commands for binding Tcl code and/or
ADP pages to URL patterns\&.
.PP
The request lifecycle consists of three primary phases: the
receive phase, the processing phase, and the delivery phase\&. The
commands detailed in this section focus on the processing phase, which
is subdivided into several stages: pre-authorization, authorization,
post-authorization, the request handler, and finally a trace stage\&.
.PP
IMAGE: naviserver-request-processing NaviServer Request Processing
.PP
Figure 1: Request processing (Threads and Request Handling Stages)
.PP
The functions in chain depicted in Figure 1 chart can be scripted or implemented in C and are
distinguished by the following properties:
.IP \(bu
Filter Chains:
.RS
.IP \(bu
Processed sequentially
.IP \(bu
Wild-card match for filter procs based on method + path
.IP \(bu
Multiple matches in the filter chain possible
.IP \(bu
Result of proc determines whether to continue or terminate chain
.IP \(bu
Can send response to the client (e\&.g\&. redirects)
.IP \(bu
registered with \fBns_register_filter\fR,
\fBns_register_shortcut_filter\fR, \fBns_register_trace\fR
.RE
.IP \(bu
Response Authorization Chain:
.RS
.IP \(bu
Registered procs are processed sequentually
.IP \(bu
Procs can use \fBns_urlspace\fR based on HTTP method and URL
path to claim responsibility
.IP \(bu
Result of proc determines whether to continue or terminate chain
.IP \(bu
Can send response to the client (e\&.g\&. redirects)
.IP \(bu
registered with \fBns_register_auth request\fR,
.RE
.IP \(bu
Request procs:
.RS
.IP \(bu
One proc per HTTP method + URL path
.IP \(bu
Proc is determined via \fIurlspace\fR
.IP \(bu
Proc performs typically heavy work (SQL, ADP, …)
.IP \(bu
Typically sends response for the request to the client
(HTTP status code, response headers and content)
.IP \(bu
Registered with \fBns_register_adp\fR,
\fBns_register_proc\fR, \fBns_register_fastpath\fR, \fBns_register_tcl\fR
.RE
.PP
.PP
The results of the procs in a chain determine, whether the chain
should be continue, terminated and proceed to the next stage, or
whether it should terminate the processing of the request completely\&.
The return code of these procs is interpreted as follows:
.IP \(bu
TCL_ERROR: When the filter procs raises an error\&.
.IP \(bu
TCL_OK: When the script is executed successfully
and ends with \fBreturn -code continue\fR (filters allow
also \fBreturn filter_ok\fR)\&.
.IP \(bu
TCL_BREAK: When the script is executed successfully
and ends with \fBreturn -code break\fR
(filters allow also \fBreturn filter_break\fR)\&.
.IP \(bu
TCL_RETURN: When the script is executed successfully
and ends with \fBreturn -code return\fR
(filters allow also  \fBreturn filter_return\fR)\&.
.PP
.PP
IMAGE: naviserver-chains NaviServer Stages and Continuations
.PP
Figure 2: Continuations and Stages
.PP
The following commands explain the details of the registration functions\&.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBns_register_adp\fR ?\fB-constraints \fIconstraints\fR\fR? ?\fB-noinherit\fR? ?\fB-expires \fItime\fR\fR? ?\fB-options autoabort|detailerror|displayerror|expire|cache|safe|singlescript|stricterror|trace|trimspace|stream\fR? ?--? \fImethod\fR \fIurl\fR ?\fIfile\fR?
Requests matching the specified HTTP \fImethod\fR and \fIurl\fR will
trigger the ADP page request handler\&. If \fIfile\fR is provided, that
file is used as the specific ADP page for all matching requests;
otherwise, the ADP page is determined at request time using
\fBns_url2file\fR\&.
.sp
This command functions similarly to \fBns_register_tcl\fR but
registers an ADP file instead of a Tcl script\&.
.sp
Use \fBns_unregister_op\fR to unregister an ADP request (described below)\&.
.TP
\fBns_register_auth\fR ?\fB-authority \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-first\fR? ?--? request|user \fIscript\fR ?\fIarg \&.\&.\&.\fR?
Registers a Tcl-based authorization handler in the NaviServer auth chain\&.
The first positional argument selects which chain to extend:
.RS
.IP \(bu
"request" - run for each HTTP request (arguments: method, URL, user, password, peer)
.IP \(bu
"user"    - run for each username/password check (arguments: user, password)
.RE
.sp
If \fB-authority\fR is provided, its value is recorded as
the handler’s label (visible e\&.g\&. via \fBns_auth … -dict\fR);
otherwise the command name passed as \fIscript\fR is used\&. Any
additional words after \fIscript\fR are passed along as extra
arguments to the callback when it is invoked\&.
.sp
If the \fB-first\fR flag is used, the authorization handler
is added at the beginning of the filter list; otherwise, it is
appended to the end\&.
This logic of authorization chains is very similar to the handling of the filter chain
(see \fBns_register_filter\fR)\&.
.sp
The function can signal the result of authorization by
returning one of the following status codes:
.RS
.IP \(bu
OK            - access granted
.IP \(bu
ERROR         - internal error during authorization
.IP \(bu
FORBIDDEN     - access denied, no possible retry
.IP \(bu
UNAUTHORIZED  - authentication required or failed, retry possible
.RE
.IP
Furthermore, the functions can decide via the Tcl return code
(\fBreturn\fR, \fBbreak\fR or \fBcontinue\fR) whether further
authorization handlers (in case these are registered) or the request
handler should be executed\&.
.CS


 # Register a user authorization handler
 ns_register_auth user ::authuser

 # Define a minimal user authorization handler, that accepts a constant password\&.
 # "-code break" prevents further authorization handlers to be called\&.
 proc ::authuser {user passwd} {
   return -code break [expr {$passwd eq "x" ? "ok" : "forbidden"}]
 }

.CE
.CS


 # Register a request authorization handler
 ns_register_auth user ::authrequest

 proc ::authrequest {method url user passwd peer} {
   # very similar minimal logic similar to ::authuser
   if {$passwd eq ""} {
      return -code break unauthorized
   }
   return -code break [expr {$passwd eq "x" ? "ok" : "forbidden"}]
 }

.CE
.IP
A slightly more elaborate example is provided in the documentation of the
\fInsladp\fR [https://github\&.com/naviserver-project/nsldap] module\&.
.sp
Once registered, these callbacks will be invoked
.RS
.IP \(bu
by the server, when a request is issued, or
.IP \(bu
by the server, when a C module calls
\fBNs_AuthorizeRequest()\fR or \fBNs_AuthorizeUser()\fR, or
.IP \(bu
by the \fBns_auth\fR command:
.RS
.IP \(bu
\fBns_auth request\fR ?\fB-dict\fR? ?--?
\fImethod\fR \fIurl\fR \fIauthuser\fR \fIauthpasswd\fR ?\fIipaddr\fR?
.IP \(bu
\fBns_auth user\fR ?\fB-dict\fR? ?--?
\fIusername\fR \fIpassword\fR
.RE
.RE
.TP
\fBns_register_fastpath\fR ?\fB-constraints \fIconstraints\fR\fR? ?\fB-noinherit\fR? ?--? \fImethod\fR \fIurl\fR
Registers the specified \fIurl\fR to be processed by the fast path
subsystem\&. All matching requests will be served by the
corresponding \&.adp file if it can be resolved\&. This option is useful
when no global fast path handler is installed\&.
.sp
Note: The \fImethod\fR argument is limited for this command to GET, POST, or HEAD\&.
.TP
\fBns_register_filter\fR ?\fB-constraints \fIconstraints\fR\fR? ?\fB-first\fR? ?--? preauth|postauth|trace \fImethod\fR \fIurlpattern\fR \fIscript\fR ?\fIarg \&.\&.\&.\fR?
Registers a Tcl filter script for the specified HTTP method and URL
pattern\&. When a request matches the given method and URL (using
glob-style matching), the registered \fIscript\fR is invoked\&. The
script is called with an extra argument indicating the filter
stage, followed by any additional arguments specified at
registration\&. If the \fB-first\fR flag is used, the filter is
added at the beginning of the filter list; otherwise, it is appended
to the end\&.
.sp
The first positional argument specifies the stage at which the filter is called:
.RS
.IP \(bu
\fBpreauth\fR - called before authorization\&.
.IP \(bu
\fBpostauth\fR - called after successful authorization\&.
.IP \(bu
\fBtrace\fR - called after the HTTP response has been sent and the connection closed\&.
.RE
.sp
The filter is executed at the designated stage if the connection's
method and URL match the provided pattern\&. For example, the following
URL patterns are valid:
.CS


 /employees/*\&.tcl
 /accounts/*/out

.CE
.sp
.sp
A filter procedure returns a value along with a Tcl return code\&. The
return code is interpreted as follows:
.RS
.IP \(bu
TCL_ERROR: When the filter procs raises an error\&.
.IP \(bu
TCL_OK: When the script is executed successfully
and ends with \fBreturn -code continue\fR or \fBreturn filter_ok\fR\&.
.IP \(bu
TCL_BREAK: When the script is executed successfully
and ends with \fBreturn -code break\fR or \fBreturn filter_break\fR\&.
.IP \(bu
TCL_RETURN: When the script is executed successfully
and ends with \fBreturn -code return\fR or \fBreturn filter_return\fR\&.
.RE
.IP
The behavior of filters depends on their type:
.RS
.TP
preauth
With pre-authorization, the filter is invoked just before
authorization\&. If the filter returns:
.RS
.IP \(bu
TCL_OK: The server proceeds to the next pre-authorization
filter, or if none remain, continues to authorization\&.
.IP \(bu
TCL_BREAK: No further pre-authorization filters are
processed; the server continues to authorization\&.
.IP \(bu
TCL_RETURN: The server closes the connection without
further processing of pre-authorization filters or the main
request handler, but trace functions (e\&.g\&., logging) will still
run\&.
.RE
.TP
postauth
With post-authorization, the filter is invoked after successful
authorization\&. If the filter returns:
.RS
.IP \(bu
TCL_OK: The server proceeds to the next
post-authorization filter, or if none remain, calls the registered
request handler\&.
.IP \(bu
TCL_BREAK: No further post-authorization filters are
processed; the registered request handler is called\&.
.IP \(bu
TCL_RETURN: The server closes the connection, skipping
further post-authorization filters and the request handler, while
still running any trace functions\&. It is assumed that the filter
has already sent an appropriate response to the client\&.
.RE
.TP
trace
Trace filters are invoked after the request has been processed, the
HTTP response sent, and the connection closed\&. If a trace filter
returns:
.RS
.IP \(bu
TCL_OK: The server proceeds to the next trace filter\&.
.IP \(bu
TCL_BREAK or TCL_RETURN: No further trace filters are executed\&.
.RE
.RE
.IP
Note: While \fBns_register_filter\fR and \fBns_register_proc\fR both
register handlers for matching URLs, they differ significantly\&. With
\fBns_register_proc\fR, the registered URL matches the specified URL
and all URLs beneath it in the hierarchy; wildcards such as "*" are
only effective on the final URL component (e\&.g\&.,
`/scripts/*\&.tcl`)\&. In contrast, filters use standard string-matching
rules and multiple filters canmatch and be invoked for a single
request\&.
.sp
Warning: Repeated execution of the same filter registration command
(e\&.g\&., during Tcl reinitialization) will result in duplicate
registrations and therefore duplicate executions\&. It is recommended
to manage registrations using a shared variable to avoid
duplicates\&. (Note that repeated executions of \fBns_register_proc\fR
with the same method/URL pattern will not cause duplicate
registrations\&.)
.sp
The filter chain can be also terminated by a \fBns_shortcut_filter\fR\&.
.TP
\fBns_register_proc\fR ?\fB-constraints \fIconstraints\fR\fR? ?\fB-noinherit\fR? ?--? \fImethod\fR \fIurl\fR \fIscript\fR ?\fIarg \&.\&.\&.\fR?
Registers a Tcl procedure or script to handle requests matching the
specified HTTP \fImethod\fR and \fIurl\fR\&. When a request matches,
the registered procedure is invoked with the arguments provided at
registration\&. If \fIscript\fR is a single Tcl word (without spaces),
it is interpreted as the name of a Tcl procedure\&.
.sp
If the \fB-noinherit\fR flag is used, the request URL must exactly
match the specified URL\&. For example, if you register a handler with
\fB/foo/bar\fR using \fB-noinherit\fR, it will only be invoked for a
request exactly matching \fB/foo/bar\fR\&.
.sp
Without \fB-noinherit\fR, the request URL may match the specified
URL or any URL below it\&. For instance, registering \fB/foo/bar\fR without
\fB-noinherit\fR will match \fB/foo/bar\fR, \fBfoo/bar/hmm\fR, and any
other URL beneath \fB/foo/bar\fR, provided no other procedure is
registered for a closer match\&.
.sp
To enable filename inheritance, include a glob-style wildcard\&. For
example, to have \fB/foo/bar\fR match \fB/foo/bar\&.html\fR,
register the URL as:
.CS


 ns_register_proc /foo/bar* \&.\&.\&.

.CE
.IP
You can register two procedures for the same HTTP method and URL by
invoking \fBns_register_proc\fR twice - once with \fB-noinherit\fR
and once without\&. Only one procedure will be invoked for a given
request, depending on whether the URL is an exact match or an
inherited match\&. For example:
.CS


 ns_register_proc -noinherit GET /foo/bar A
 ns_register_proc GET /foo/bar B
 ns_register_proc GET /foo/bar/hmm C

.CE
.IP
In this scenario, \fBA\fR is called only when the request URL
is exactly \fB/foo/bar\fR;
\fBB\fR is invoked for URLs under \fB/foo/bar\fR (if no closer match exists);
and \fBC\fR is called when the request URL is \fB/foo/bar/hmm\fR or beneath\&.
.sp
The following example demonstrates how to pass arguments at
registration time to the registered Tcl command:
.CS


 ns_register_proc GET /noargs noargs
 ns_register_proc GET /onearg onearg 1
 ns_register_proc GET /twoargs twoargs 1 2
 ns_register_proc GET /threeargs threeargs 1 2 3

 proc noargs { } {
     ns_returnnotice 200 "testing" "noargs"
  }

 proc onearg { x } {
     ns_returnnotice 200 "testing" "onearg gets $x"
 }

 proc twoargs { x y } {
     ns_returnnotice 200 "testing" "twoargs gets $x $y "
 }

 proc threeargs { x y z } {
     ns_returnnotice 200 "testing" "threeargs gets $x $y $z"
 }

.CE
.TP
\fBns_register_proxy\fR \fImethod\fR \fIprotocol\fR \fIscript\fR ?\fIarg \&.\&.\&.\fR?
Registers a Tcl script as a handler for the specified method/protocol
combination\&. By registering this command, NaviServer acts as a plain
HTTP proxy server (also called a "forward proxy"), which handles
outbound traffic on behalf of clients, shielding the client’s identity
or controlling outbound traffic\&.  This is different from a reverse
proxy server, which is managing traffic towards internal servers (see
\fIrevproxy\fR [\&.\&./\&.\&./revproxy/files/revproxy\&.html])\&.
.sp
Example of a trivial HTTP proxy server (Note: there is a much more elaborate
reverse proxy server available as NaviServer module, which might
serve as a model for this forward proxy)\&.
.CS


 ns_register_proxy GET http http_proxy_handler

 proc http_proxy_handler { args } {
     #
     # Get the full URL from request line
     #
     if {![regexp {^\\S+\\s(\\S\&.+)\\s\\S+$} [ns_conn request] \&. URL]} {
         ns_log warning "proxy: request line malformed: <[ns_conn request]>"
         ns_return 400 text/plain "invalid proxy request"
     } else {
         #
         # Run the request
         #
         set d [ns_http run $URL]
         set content_type [ns_set get -nocase [dict get $d headers] content-type]
         ns_return [[dict get $d status] $content_type [dict get $d body]]
     }
 }

.CE
.TP
\fBns_register_tcl\fR ?\fB-constraints \fIconstraints\fR\fR? ?\fB-noinherit\fR? ?--? \fImethod\fR \fIurl\fR ?\fIfile\fR?
Registers a Tcl file to be invoked when a request matches the
specified HTTP \fImethod\fR and \fIurl\fR\&. This command is typically
used for extension-less URLs or for mapping physical files to virtual
URLs\&. It works similarly to \fBns_register_adp\fR, except that the
file to be evaluated should be a Tcl script capable of generating a
response using commands such as \fBns_return\fR or \fBns_write\fR\&.
.sp
If the \fIfile\fR argument is omitted, the url2file mapping
is used the determine a filename registered for the URL\&.
.sp
Use \fBns_unregister_op\fR to unregister a Tcl request handler\&.
.TP
\fBns_register_trace\fR ?\fB-constraints \fIconstraints\fR\fR? \fImethod\fR \fIurlpattern\fR \fIscript\fR ?\fIarg \&.\&.\&.\fR?
Registers a Tcl script as a trace filter for the specified HTTP
\fImethod\fR and \fIurlpattern\fR\&. Trace filters are executed after the
main request and are only invoked for successful requests (i\&.e\&., no
server errors occur)\&.
.sp
A filter registered this way behaves exactly like a filter registered
via \fBns_register_filter trace\fR, except that on the for the
registered command, no reason code is added as the first argument
when the trace filter is called, and its result is not subject to the
special result handling\&.
.TP
\fBns_register_url2file\fR ?\fB-noinherit\fR? ?--? \fIurl\fR \fIscript\fR ?\fIarg \&.\&.\&.\fR?
.TP
\fBns_register_fasturl2file\fR ?\fB-noinherit\fR? ?--? \fIurl\fR ?\fIbasepath\fR?
These commands are used for runtime resolution of requested URLs to
corresponding files that will be served to the client\&. They utilize
the \fBns_url2file\fR interface to resolve the file associated with
the current URL\&. The \fBns_register_fasturl2file\fR command registers
the default fast url2file procedure for the given \fIurl\fR\&. The Tcl
\fIscript\fR provided to \fBns_register_url2file\fR is expected to
return the full path corresponding to the requested URL\&.
.TP
\fBns_shortcut_filter\fR ?\fB-constraints \fIconstraints\fR\fR? preauth|postauth|trace \fImethod\fR \fIurlpattern\fR
Registers a special "shortcut" filter that cancels further of a
filter chain\&. Once this shortcut filter was called, any subsequent
filters matching the specified stage, HTTP method, and URL pattern
are bypassed\&. This mechanism is particularly useful for efficiently
preventing unnecessary filter execution when a terminating condition
has been met\&.
.sp
The shortcut filter functions identically to a standard filter that
returns TCL_BREAK, but it is implemented more efficiently\&.
.TP
\fBns_unregister_op\fR ?\fB-allconstraints\fR? ?\fB-noinherit\fR? ?\fB-recurse\fR? ?\fB-server \fIserver\fR\fR? ?--? \fImethod\fR \fIurl\fR
Unregisters a request handler for the specified HTTP
\fImethod\fR and \fIurl\fR combination\&. This command removes any Tcl or C
functions previously registered for this method/URL pair that share
the same inheritance setting\&. The command unregisters commands
request handlers registered e\&.g\&. with the following commands:
\fBns_register_adp\fR,
\fBns_register_cgi\fR,
\fBns_register_fastpath\fR, and
\fBns_register_proc\fR\&.
.sp
The option \fB-allconstraints\fR implies that all context
constraints are deleted\&. When the option \fB-noinherit\fR is used,
only the values set with \fB-noinherit\fR are deleted, and vice
versa\&.
.sp
The \fB-server\fR parameter specifies the (virtual) server from
which the handler should be unregistered\&. If omitted, the current
server is assumed\&.
.TP
\fBns_unregister_url2file\fR ?\fB-noinherit\fR? ?\fB-recurse\fR? ?\fB-server \fIserver\fR\fR? ?--? \fIurl\fR
Unregisters a URL-to-file resolver previously registered with
\fBns_register_url2file\fR\&. The \fB-noinherit\fR flag and
\fB-server\fR parameter function as described before\&.
.PP
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB-constraints\fR \fIconstraints\fR
allows you to povide
additional context constraints to specify, when this handler should
be invoked (for details see
\fIContext Constraints\fR [\&./ns_urlspace\&.html#section5])\&.
.TP
\fB-cache\fR \fIt\fR
Specifies for how long to keep result in the cache, in seconds or absolute time\&.
.TP
\fB-expires\fR \fIt\fR
Specifies that the result of evaluating the \fIADP\fR page should be cached
for \fIt\fR seconds (or a value with a time unit)\&.
If  \fIt\fR is large, it expresses a time in the
future (expressed as seconds after the \fIepoch\fR)\&.
.TP
\fB-noinherit\fR
Specifies that the directory portion of the request URL should match
exactly and not also apply to a URL lower in the hierarchy\&. This is
the default\&.
.sp
Different \fIADP\fR page handlers can be registered for the
same \fImethod\fR and \fIurl\fR if one uses the ?-noinherit?
option and the other does not\&.
.CS


 \fBns_register_adp\fR            GET /a x\&.adp
 \fBns_register_adp\fR -noinherit GET /b y\&.adp

.CE
.IP
In the previous example, a\&.adp will run for the requests /a, /a/b,
/a/b/c and so on\&. But b\&.adp will only run when the requested URL is
exactly /b\&. The request /b/c will result in a 404 Page Not Found error\&.
.CS


 \fBns_register_adp\fR            GET /a x\&.adp
 \fBns_register_adp\fR -noinherit GET /a y\&.adp

.CE
.IP
In this example, a request for the exact URL /a will run the y\&.adp page, while
/a/b and /a/b/c will run the x\&.adp page\&.  The y\&.adp page is more specific
than the x\&.adp page, and hides it for the /a request, which matched it exactly\&.
.TP
\fB-options\fR \fIoptions\fR
A list of one or more \fIADP\fR options that should be added to the default
set which are in effect while the \fIADP\fR page runs\&. Options can only be
enabled, not disabled\&. Valid options are those that \fBns_adp_ctl\fR
accepts, which can be used by \fIADP\fR code to fine tune the options\&.
.TP
\fB-recurse\fR
if specified, the delete operation will be recursive\&.
.PP
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
string \fImethod\fR
The HTTP \fImethod\fR, such as \fIHEAD\fR, \fIGET\fR or \fIPOST\fR,
which will be compared using exact string equality to the HTTP request\&.
.CS


 foreach method {HEAD GET POST} {
     \fBns_register_adp\fR $method /foo/bar "\fIhmm\&.adp\fR"
 }

.CE
.TP
string \fIurl\fR
The \fIurl\fR pattern to match against the URL in each HTTP request\&. The last
component of the URL may contain the globbing characters \fI*\fR and \fI?\fR\&.
.CS


 \fBns_register_adp\fR GET /dynamic/*\&.htm?

.CE
.IP
In this example, a URL where the last component matches the pattern *\&.htm?,
such as /dynamic/foo\&.htm or /dynamic/a/b/c/bar\&.html, will be run by the
ADP engine\&.
.sp
Only the last component of the URL may be a pattern\&. If this is too restrictive,
try using \fBns_register_filter\fR\&.
.TP
string \fI?file?\fR
The optional argument \fIfile\fR is either an absolute path to a file in the filesystem,
or a path relative to the page root directory of the virtual server\&.
.CS


 \fBns_register_adp\fR GET /a "\fIa\&.adp\fR"    ; # relative to page root
 \fBns_register_adp\fR GET /b "\fIb/b\&.adp\fR"  ; # relative to page root
 \fBns_register_adp\fR GET /c "\fI/c\&.adp\fR"   ; # absolute in file-system root

.CE
.IP
The first and second lines specify a file relative to the page
root directory\&. The full path is constructed each time the \fIADP\fR page is
requested using \fBns_pagepath\fR, so \fBns_serverroot\fR and
\fBns_register_url2file\fR callbacks will be taken into account\&.
.sp
In the third example, a specific \fIADP\fR file in the server's filesystem
is registered for a similarly named URL (and all URLs below
it)\&. There are no calls to \fBns_pagepath\fR or \fBns_url2file\fR
during the request\&.
.PP
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
In the following example, all files with the \&.adp extension in and below the
/big URL should be served by the \fIADP\fR handler, with the options
\fIstricterror\fR and \fIstream\fR enabled:
.CS


 foreach method {GET HEAD POST} {
     \fBns_register_adp\fR -options {stream stricterror} -- $method /big/*\&.adp
 }

.CE
.PP
In the following example, we return for every GET request
for a \&.php file in the given path an info message\&.
.CS


 ns_register_proc GET /foo/bar/*\&.php {
   ns_return 200 text/plain "Server refuses to execute PHP scripts here"
 }

.CE
.PP
This example shows how to expire all HTML files after an hour:
.CS


 # Use nsv to avoid double registration of a filter, when the
 # file containing the registration is called twice
 if {![nsv_exists filters installed]} {
   nsv_set filters installed 1
   ns_register_filter postauth GET /*\&.html ExpireSoon 3600
 }

 proc ExpireSoon {why seconds} {
   ns_set update [ns_conn outputheaders] Expires [ns_httptime [expr {$seconds + [ns_time]}]]
   return -code continue
 }

.CE
.PP
The command \fBns_server\fR can be used to list the
currently registered \fIfilters\fR, \fItraces\fR or \fIprocs\fR\&.
.CS


  \fBns_server\fR ?\fB-server \fIserver\fR\fR? \fBauthprocs\fR
  \fBns_server\fR ?\fB-server \fIserver\fR\fR? \fBfilters\fR
  \fBns_server\fR ?\fB-server \fIserver\fR\fR? \fBrequestprocs\fR
  \fBns_server\fR ?\fB-server \fIserver\fR\fR? \fBtraces\fR

.CE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ns_adp, ns_adp_register, ns_auth, ns_conn, ns_http, ns_server, ns_time
.SH KEYWORDS
ADP, callback, context constraints, fastpath, filter, forwarding proxy, handler, proxy, request, request authentication, server built-in, urlspace, user authentication
